  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "plessy",
      "title": "Plessy: the accepted distinction",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "664046fcbd801f304e27f84ab8ed891a16054af8e767ccb527de0ff73e882a1e",
          "sha256": "854f8f624947bf0a09091cafb5cdf1126cb20f840a68051d7746d7600966c609",
          "page": 1
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The reading describes a challenge to Louisiana’s Separate Car Act. Plessy deliberately sat in a railway car reserved for white passengers, refused to move and was arrested. His argument connected enforced racial separation to unequal treatment under the Fourteenth Amendment.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "664046fcbd801f304e27f84ab8ed891a16054af8e767ccb527de0ff73e882a1e",
              "sha256": "854f8f624947bf0a09091cafb5cdf1126cb20f840a68051d7746d7600966c609",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The majority treated separation as a matter of social equality distinct from civil and political rights. It accepted separate facilities if supposedly equal. Harlan dissented. This describes the historical majority’s reasoning, not an endorsement of it.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "664046fcbd801f304e27f84ab8ed891a16054af8e767ccb527de0ff73e882a1e",
              "sha256": "854f8f624947bf0a09091cafb5cdf1126cb20f840a68051d7746d7600966c609",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "key",
          "text": "Do not infer a vote count just from nine seats or from the worksheet’s wording about a sole dissenter. The source is not sufficient for that arithmetic.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "664046fcbd801f304e27f84ab8ed891a16054af8e767ccb527de0ff73e882a1e",
              "sha256": "854f8f624947bf0a09091cafb5cdf1126cb20f840a68051d7746d7600966c609",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "brown",
      "title": "Brown: why separation itself mattered",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "066033f6cb64bd8f48d8713732f2714a6cb44dedb818643054af79791ae1c476",
          "sha256": "2f6241b263607a327a100be465435cd6dca2786901ffc87d2e20cb814461f6f4",
          "page": 1
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The Brown reading describes parents challenging segregated public education. The argument was not only that buildings or resources differed: enforced separation itself communicated inferiority and damaged equal educational opportunity.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "066033f6cb64bd8f48d8713732f2714a6cb44dedb818643054af79791ae1c476",
              "sha256": "2f6241b263607a327a100be465435cd6dca2786901ffc87d2e20cb814461f6f4",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The Supreme Court agreed that segregation in public schools denied equal protection. The later conflict over university enrolment in the reading illustrates that a legal decision and its practical implementation are different stages.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "066033f6cb64bd8f48d8713732f2714a6cb44dedb818643054af79791ae1c476",
              "sha256": "2f6241b263607a327a100be465435cd6dca2786901ffc87d2e20cb814461f6f4",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "comparison",
      "title": "Same constitutional language, changed precedent",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "id": "664046fcbd801f304e27f84ab8ed891a16054af8e767ccb527de0ff73e882a1e",
          "sha256": "854f8f624947bf0a09091cafb5cdf1126cb20f840a68051d7746d7600966c609",
          "page": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "066033f6cb64bd8f48d8713732f2714a6cb44dedb818643054af79791ae1c476",
          "sha256": "2f6241b263607a327a100be465435cd6dca2786901ffc87d2e20cb814461f6f4",
          "page": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "e4ae83cbce6ec3b55d9fe5d0a998b504f535ca5a93b2b2d955f767ce42fdfa25",
          "sha256": "bb2abf6257b0b955958b5cd99596ae1ff9bd524e90459c129dc96a2a01d75fb5",
          "page": 1
        }
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Plessy and Brown both concern equality, but they concern different settings: railway cars and public schools. Brown rejected the separate-but-equal approach in education; it was not an appeal by the Brown families within Plessy’s original lawsuit.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "664046fcbd801f304e27f84ab8ed891a16054af8e767ccb527de0ff73e882a1e",
              "sha256": "854f8f624947bf0a09091cafb5cdf1126cb20f840a68051d7746d7600966c609",
              "page": 1
            },
            {
              "id": "066033f6cb64bd8f48d8713732f2714a6cb44dedb818643054af79791ae1c476",
              "sha256": "2f6241b263607a327a100be465435cd6dca2786901ffc87d2e20cb814461f6f4",
              "page": 1
            },
            {
              "id": "e4ae83cbce6ec3b55d9fe5d0a998b504f535ca5a93b2b2d955f767ce42fdfa25",
              "sha256": "bb2abf6257b0b955958b5cd99596ae1ff9bd524e90459c129dc96a2a01d75fb5",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The vocabulary task asks you to define segregation, facilities, precedent, suing and appealing in sentences tied to the cases. A strong explanation connects facts to the constitutional question, the court’s reasoning and the consequence for future cases.",
          "source_refs": [
            {
              "id": "e4ae83cbce6ec3b55d9fe5d0a998b504f535ca5a93b2b2d955f767ce42fdfa25",
              "sha256": "bb2abf6257b0b955958b5cd99596ae1ff9bd524e90459c129dc96a2a01d75fb5",
              "page": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
